@node Security Work
@chapter Security Work
@cindex security work
@cindex codebud-sec

Codebud's security layer (@file{codebud_sec}, part of the Codebud
distribution) is for @emph{authorized} security work: incident response
on hosts you own or are authorized to examine, and penetration tests you
are contracted to run.  It adds an engagement file that states what was
authorized, a command, @command{codebud-sec}, that keeps every step to
it, and an evidence log that records every step.  Nothing here grants
access to anything; it only states, and helps keep to, what was already
authorized.  Use it only within a written authorization (rules of
engagement, a signed scope, an incident ticket).

@menu
* Security Work at a Glance::   What codebud-sec adds to codebud.
* The Engagement File::         Client, scope, window, fence.
* Running codebud-sec::         Sessions, evidence, pinning.
* The Evidence Log::            What is recorded and how to check it.
* What Security Work Does Not Cover::  The limits, plainly.
@end menu


@node Security Work at a Glance
@section Security Work at a Glance

@multitable @columnfractions .32 .68
@headitem Piece @tab What it does
@item Security profiles
@tab @code{security-ir} (incident response: read-only triage and
forensics, no network) and @code{security-pentest} (reconnaissance and
scanning of in-scope hosts); @pxref{Profiles}.
@item Engagement file
@tab A TOML file naming the client, the in-scope and excluded targets and
the time window.
@item Scope guard
@tab Reads every host a network step will contact from its command line
and refuses the step when one is out of scope, excluded, or the window
is closed (lookups out of scope ask instead).  A command it cannot read
is refused.
@item Network fence
@tab Runs scanning steps where the network itself lets through only
their targets (@pxref{Isolated Steps}).
@item Evidence log
@tab An encrypted, hash-chained, append-only record of every step, before
it runs and after it ends, refused steps too.
@item Integrity
@tab Both profiles refuse to start without a pinned manifest of the
models and programs they run (@pxref{Integrity Checks}).
@end multitable

The security profiles need an encryption key (@command{codebud key
init}) and a pinned manifest (@command{codebud-sec pin}).
@code{security-pentest} runs no command until an engagement is open, so
it is used through @command{codebud-sec}.


@node The Engagement File
@section The Engagement File
@cindex engagement file

@example
client = "ACME Ltd"
ticket = "PT-2026-042"
operator = "A. Analyst"
in_scope = ["10.20.0.0/16", "app.acme.example", "2001:db8:42::/48"]
out_of_scope = ["10.20.5.0/24", "admin.app.acme.example"]
starts = 2026-11-16T08:00:00Z
ends = 2026-11-20T18:00:00Z
fence_scanning = true
notes = "Production: no denial of service; scans 08:00-18:00 only."
@end example

@table @code
@item in_scope
Networks (CIDR), addresses and names.  A name covers its subdomains
(@code{app.acme.example} covers @code{api.app.acme.example}).  Required.
@item out_of_scope
Excluded networks, addresses and names; they win over @code{in_scope}.
A network is in scope only when all of it is.
@item starts, ends
The window the work is authorized for (UTC unless an offset is given).
Outside it, active steps are refused.
@item fence_scanning
@code{true} (the default) runs scanning steps behind the network fence;
@code{false} gives raw scans (nmap SYN, UDP and OS scans, masscan) back,
with the scope guard as their only check.
@item client, ticket, operator, notes
Recorded in the evidence log.
@end table

A name is never matched against an address, and the guard never
resolves a name to decide scope; the fence resolves the names a step
uses just before it runs.


@node Running codebud-sec
@section Running codebud-sec

@example
codebud-sec chat --engagement acme.toml
codebud-sec run  --engagement acme.toml "scan the web tier for open ports"
codebud-sec plan --engagement acme.toml --profile security-ir "..."
@end example

@noindent
@command{chat}, @command{run} and @command{plan} open the engagement and
then run codebud itself, with the @code{security-pentest} profile unless
@option{--profile} names another.  Any other option is passed on to
codebud (@option{--resume}, @option{-m}, @dots{}).  For the whole session
the scope guard and the network fence are active and every step is
recorded; when it ends they are taken down.  Outside the window,
@command{codebud-sec} says so at the start and active steps are refused.

@example
codebud-sec evidence add disk.E01 rules-of-engagement.pdf \
    -m "image received from the client" --engagement acme.toml
codebud-sec evidence verify [--files] --engagement acme.toml
codebud-sec evidence show --engagement acme.toml
codebud-sec evidence close --engagement acme.toml
codebud-sec pin
@end example

@noindent
@command{evidence add} records files with their SHA-256 (and a note).
@command{verify} checks the hash chain, and with @option{--files} that
the recorded files are unchanged.  @command{show} lists the entries.
@command{close} ends the log: nothing can be added after it.
@command{pin} is @command{codebud integrity pin}.  @option{--evidence
LOG} names a log other than the default, @file{<engagement>.evidence.jsonl}
beside the engagement file.


@node The Evidence Log
@section The Evidence Log
@cindex evidence log

The log is a JSON Lines file, one entry per line, each holding the hash
of the one before; editing, removing or reordering an entry breaks the
chain.  The first entry names the engagement file with its SHA-256, so
the log says exactly what was authorized.  Each session adds a
@code{start} and an @code{end} note; each step a @code{step} entry before
it runs (the tool, its arguments, the description) and one after it ends
(its exit status and output); a refused step a @code{warning} with the
reason.  A step whose start cannot be recorded does not run.  Output or
arguments longer than 64 KiB are kept as their SHA-256, size and
beginning.

The log is encrypted at rest with your key: each entry is sealed, and
only its sequence number, kind and the two hashes stay readable, so that
appending needs no passphrase.  Reading and a full @command{verify} ask
for it.

@cindex head hash
What the chain proves is relative to its head: anyone who can write the
file could rewrite it consistently.  @command{verify} prints the head
hash --- record it outside the log (the case notes, a ticket, a signed
message), and the log then shows any change made before that point.


@node What Security Work Does Not Cover
@section What Security Work Does Not Cover

@itemize
@item Authorization.  The engagement file states it; it does not grant
it, and Codebud cannot tell whether it is true.
@item Names.  The client's DNS decides what an in-scope name resolves to,
and an address shared with other sites (a CDN) is reachable while a step
that names it runs.
@item Lookups.  @command{dig}, @command{host} and @command{whois} are not
fenced; out of scope they ask rather than refuse.
@item Raw scans.  With @code{fence_scanning = false}, scanning relies on
the scope guard reading the command line.
@item The machine itself.  The evidence log and the integrity checks
detect changes made through Codebud and to the files they cover; a
compromised operating system can defeat both (RFC 3227, section 5).
@end itemize
